claim:
needs: 26
title: Validate field values, and decide what the schema file becomes
effort: M
area: format
created: 2026-09-17T06:26:19Z
updated: 2026-09-17T06:26:19Z

The values column is documentation. Nothing checks that effort is one of S,
M, L — check enforces that a field is declared and, after 24, that a
required one has a value, but never what the value is.

Enums are the cheap half. "S, M, L" splits on commas, check reports
"effort is 'XL', which the schema does not allow", and a cell with prose in
it rather than a list — "one line: where the last agent got to" — has to
keep meaning "anything", so the column needs a way to say unconstrained.
That is the first sign the column is becoming a grammar.

Patterns are where it breaks, and the reason is the container, not the
pattern. A regex uses | for alternation and the markdown table uses | to
separate cells. Escaping is possible and takes the file out of "awk parses
it in a pinch", which is the property 26 exists to protect. The design
document already called this: the table holds while the schema stays flat
key/value, and per-value behaviour forces a real config format.

So decide the container, and this ticket is that decision:

- Keep the table and cap the grammar at enums. Cheapest, renders on a
  forge, and says patterns are not coming.
- Keep the table and escape \| in cells. Buys patterns, costs the
  no-parser property and produces cells nobody wants to read or write.
- Line-oriented stanzas in the format pidly already uses: one field per
  block, key: value lines, blank line between. The whole system then has
  one grammar, get_field-style reads work on it, and a pattern can contain
  anything but a newline. Loses the rendered table.

  field: effort
  required: yes
  values: S, M, L

The third is the one to beat. It is the format's own shape applied to the
format's own configuration, and it removes the delimiter problem instead of
escaping around it.

If patterns land, pin the dialect in the same breath — POSIX ERE, as grep -E
and awk read it — or the same schema validates differently on two machines.
And decide what check does with a pattern the local awk rejects: a broken
schema must be a finding, not a crash.

Needs 26 because the prose comes out either way, and because deciding the
container on a file that still has pidly's documentation in it means
deciding two things at once.

Done when values are enforced, the container is decided and written down in
the design with the reasoning, and the tests cover a value outside an enum,
an unconstrained field, and a schema the parser cannot read.
